1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,644
Two thousand two hundred thirty-nine tokens on one side.

2
00:00:03,644 --> 00:00:06,246
Nine hundred seventy-three on the other.

3
00:00:06,246 --> 00:00:08,068
Same Claude, same questions.

4
00:00:08,068 --> 00:00:10,020
And yet, the bill is the same.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:11,493
The skill I tested:

6
00:00:11,493 --> 00:00:11,986
caveman.

7
00:00:11,986 --> 00:00:14,208
It makes Claude talk like a caveman.

8
00:00:14,208 --> 00:00:15,689
The number going around:

9
00:00:15,689 --> 00:00:17,664
sixty-five percent fewer tokens.

10
00:00:17,664 --> 00:00:20,502
Its author pulled it from GitHub in September.

11
00:00:20,502 --> 00:00:21,860
I ran the test myself.

12
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:22,823
The test:

13
00:00:22,823 --> 00:00:24,262
six everyday questions.

14
00:00:24,262 --> 00:00:28,265
An Excel formula, a draining battery, a loan, deleting a folder,

15
00:00:28,265 --> 00:00:30,580
a four-oh-four error, a pasta recipe.

16
00:00:32,890 --> 00:00:36,903
Without the skill, Claude answers politely, with headings and lists.

17
00:00:36,903 --> 00:00:37,670
With caveman:

18
00:00:37,670 --> 00:00:40,680
short sentences, no more "Sure!", the answer first.

19
00:00:40,980 --> 00:00:42,184
On the Excel formula:

20
00:00:42,184 --> 00:00:44,878
two hundred thirty-one tokens versus sixty-six.

21
00:00:44,878 --> 00:00:48,260
Seventy-one percent less, and nothing important is missing.

22
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:51,713
But on the recipe, only thirty-eight percent less.

23
00:00:51,713 --> 00:00:53,604
A recipe is mostly quantities:

24
00:00:53,604 --> 00:00:54,550
nothing to cut.

25
00:00:54,850 --> 00:00:55,320
Overall:

26
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:56,789
fifty-seven percent less.

27
00:00:56,789 --> 00:01:00,550
The sixty-five percent only shows up on one question out of six.

28
00:01:00,850 --> 00:01:02,136
And here's the catch.

29
00:01:02,136 --> 00:01:06,301
The skill itself weighs one thousand one hundred fifty-eight tokens,

30
00:01:06,301 --> 00:01:07,709
reloaded every session.

31
00:01:07,709 --> 00:01:11,629
Over six questions, it saves one thousand two hundred sixty-six.

32
00:01:11,629 --> 00:01:12,180
Net gain:

33
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:13,160
about a hundred.

34
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:16,288
The session counter confirms it:

35
00:01:16,288 --> 00:01:19,250
sixty-two thousand five hundred ninety-one without,

36
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:21,690
sixty-two thousand six hundred three with.

37
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:21,980
Same.

38
00:01:22,350 --> 00:01:23,107
Another miss:

39
00:01:23,107 --> 00:01:26,484
to delete a folder, the caveman version keeps the warning,

40
00:01:26,484 --> 00:01:30,210
but drops the one about an extra space that can wipe everything.

41
00:01:31,880 --> 00:01:32,508
My verdict:

42
00:01:32,508 --> 00:01:35,822
useful for long coding sessions, with hundreds of replies.

43
00:01:35,822 --> 00:01:38,106
For a few questions, it changes nothing.

44
00:01:38,106 --> 00:01:40,220
And always reread dangerous commands.
